A||||7||DXR_DAD||IT LEAKS!!?
Z||000000||DXR_DAD||07-23-99||09:08 PM||||can anyone tell me the procedure for porting and re-weld on the stock exhaust manifolds for the 2.8. I`ve got that leak that everyone talks about. Don`t know if its a gasket or a crack. But if I`ve got to take them off I`ll do the port and re-weld job.<p>thanks for the help!
Z||000001||PaUL||07-23-99||10:13 PM||||The port and reweld job that you speak of doesnt really get rid of the leaks. If you remove the manifolds and look inside the ports, youll see that theres metal sticking up into the runners. These are obstructions and should be removed by grinding. The majority of exhaust leaks are caused by manifolds that don't fit flush against the head, leaving small gaps for the exhaust to leak out of. I used to be on the fiero mailing list, someone there said to use a belt sander to sand it down flat. Not having a beltsander I never tried this myself. 
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||07-23-99||10:39 PM||||Yes I will remove the obstructions, and after welding the flanges re-surface them flat (machined or belt sand them flat. How much do I grind to open the ports? or do I just gasket match?
Z||000003||lowCG||07-24-99||12:25 AM||||From what I understand,there is a weld bead left inside the manifold that's globbed into the path of the exhaust flow;if it's obvious,and big,grind away,if it's layed down pretty smooth,leave it.All this isn't going to make your car do wheelies or anything,might change the exhaust note a little.<br>I have a leak of some sort that I will be dealing with pretty soon(don't drive the car)and am thinking that I will grind the side of the weld that faces the head,just to eliminate any situation where the exhaust flow will hit a surface that's directly (flat)opposing the flow,and no more,so I won't need to weld(and heat up/make brittle)the old manifold any further.Not worth tweaking on like an intake or anything for the 2HP increase that it may achieve.
Z||000004||PaUL||07-24-99||01:21 AM||||From looking at the burn pattern on my old exhaust gaskets, I can tell you GM's idea of tolerances leaves alot to be desired. On one side one of the ports was almost a 1/4 in. off. If you are going to grind the ports, just try to match the ports on the heads as close as you can. You can also enlarge the bolt holes on the manifolds so you can adjust the position more. On the trunk side its easy but the other side is really hard.
Z||000005||Phil||07-24-99||04:34 AM||||In one of the posts about a month or so ago there was a url for the mod of the manifolds.  I did the porting thing, that stainless is hard on stones. When welding run a bead around the outside where the flanges meet the riser and grind away the inside. When I brought my manifolds to the welder I bolted them together to help further distortion. Most automotive machine shops have a giant belt sander for heads- give them a few bucks to sand your manifolds flat. The whole deal will run under $50
Z||000006||DXR_DAD||07-24-99||11:28 PM||||Thanx Phil, <br>I found a Tech-Tip from Fiero Focus On Line: "V6 Exhaust manifold repair & porting". It gave me most of the info I needed.<p>Did it make a big differance in the sound?<br>Is the rest of your exhaust system stock?
Z||000007||Phil||07-25-99||07:23 AM||||No<br>Yes
